 "title": "Closing open ends seals or collapses space",
 "subject": "Why local growth rules that close open ends cannot grow a space with a growing ruler",
 "conclusion": "In hub-free parity laws bond distance is the least two-sided boundary, counting seams and open ends held inside, so every such boundary is a knot. A region without open ends behind one seam pins it and no knot crosses, and a long region with a short boundary keeps bond distance bounded; closing rules therefore freeze space into cells or collapse it.",
